{"step_index":1619,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-02T19:51:22Z","input_tokens":2661,"cache_read_tokens":136198,"output_tokens":6764,"thinking":"The `renderReviewWorkspace` function directly calls `renderAuditScorecard` on re-render, suggesting a close relationship between the two components. Focusing on `AuditScorecard.js`, the code includes a section that handles GST presets, specifically targeting elements with the `data-gst-preset` attribute. The code extracts a percentage value from this attribute, indicating a configuration related to GST rates.\n\n\nThe code calculates tax amounts based on a rate percentage extracted from the clicked button's data attribute and a subtotal value. The subtotal is sourced from the invoice data, or falls back to an audit item sum if unavailable. The tax split calculation handles both intra-state (CGST/SGST) and inter-state (IGST) scenarios, dependent on matching the first two digits of the supplier and buyer GSTINs. Initial observation revealed the buyer GSTIN is pulled from invoice. buyer_gstin and now this variable needs further investigation to confirm that the expected value is provided.\n\n\nThe code extracts the state codes from both supplier and buyer GSTINs, crucial for determining the tax type (CGST/SGST or IGST). If `invoice.buyer_gstin` is missing, the code defaults to a predefined value based on the user prompt's example. When supplier and buyer state codes match, IGST is zero and CGST/SGST are split equally; otherwise, IGST is the full tax amount. If the supplier's state is missing, intra-state tax calculation is assumed. Examination of the UI component confirms the user's ability to potentially edit the buyer GSTIN.\n\n\nThe UI component indicates an existing input field for Supplier GSTIN validation, along with a potential for user modification.  The code suggests a pre-existing \"Buyer Details\" section, which warrants investigation for buyer GSTIN input. Reviewing the user's constraints reveals a strict limitation against modifying other backend and frontend components unrelated to GST corrections; the layout of header inputs must remain unchanged.\n\n\nThe \"Billed To\" section in the UI currently lacks the Buyer GSTIN display, which will be added directly within the `InvoiceViewer.js` component, ensuring the value is readily visible. Additionally, the `AuditScorecard.js` component will be updated to explicitly show the state code comparison between the supplier and buyer GSTINs to clarify the tax calculation logic. This visual comparison will be placed under the GST tax rate section, providing a clear reference point.\n\n\nThe code snippet confirms that the comparison of the first two digits of the Supplier and Buyer GSTIN determines the tax type (Intra-State or Inter-State) shown to the user. Clicking on the tax input fields (`input-cgst`, `input-sgst`, `input-igst`) updates the respective tax values, triggering a re-audit. Clicking a GST preset button calculates the tax amount based on the button's percentage, subtotal, and GSTIN state codes, updating `cgst`, `sgst`, or `igst` accordingly. The logic considers intra-state or inter-state scenarios based on matching or differing state codes.\n\n\nThe tax calculation logic was traced through an example scenario, confirming the correct computation of CGST, SGST, and IGST based on state code comparison. The `onUpdateHeader` function modifies the invoice's tax fields, triggering a re-audit. This re-audit recalculates tax values and compares the `total_declared` value against the calculated total to identify any discrepancies.\n\n\nThe `handleAutoFixMath()` function recalculates the subtotal based on item quantities and rates, updating `this.currentInvoice.subtotal`. This recalculation directly impacts the value used in the tax calculation, as the `subtotal` is a key component. The invoice parser logic extracts the total amount in words, which could have an effect.\n\n\nThe invoice parser uses regular expressions to extract financial figures (subtotal, CGST, SGST, IGST) from each line of text. It appears that specific patterns are used to identify and parse these values. The code attempts to identify subtotal, CGST, SGST and IGST figures based on keywords and regular expressions matching Indian currency formats.\n\n\nThe initial regex incorrectly captured extraneous numbers due to its broad matching of digits and currency symbols. The updated patterns specifically target lines containing \"CGST\", \"SGST\", or \"IGST\" along with their respective values, and use stricter currency matching. The revised regex now correctly uses named groups to identify percentage and value. A check ensures that the lines in the `summaryZone` match this more specific pattern.\n\n\nThe updated regex logic appears to correctly parse CGST, SGST, and IGST values whether the percentage is explicitly present or absent. It correctly extracts the currency value even if the percentage is missing, using the second capture group as the primary value and parsing it correctly. The extraction logic accounts for percentage-based and direct amount formats, as the regular expression captures both, along with a currency symbol. The `parseIndianCurrency` function effectively converts the extracted strings to numeric values.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python3 -c '\nimport re\n\npat = re.compile(r\"(?:CGST|Central GST)[\\s@%]*([0-9\\.]+)?%?[\\s:]*[₹\\s]*([0-9,]+(?:\\.[0-9]{2})?)\", re.I)\n\ndef parse_val(text):\n    m = pat.search(text)\n    if not m:\n        return 0\n    g1 = m.group(1)\n    g2 = m.group(2)\n    if \"%\" in text:\n        return float(g2.replace(\",\", \"\")) if g2 else 0.0\n    if g1 and g2:\n        if g2 in [\"0\", \"00\"]:\n            return float((g1 + g2).replace(\",\", \"\"))\n        return float(g2.replace(\",\", \"\")) if float(g2.replace(\",\", \"\")) > 0 else float(g1.replace(\",\", \"\"))\n    if g2:\n        return float(g2.replace(\",\", \"\"))\n    if g1:\n        return float(g1.replace(\",\", \"\"))\n    return 0.0\n\nsamples = [\n    \"CGST @ 9%: ₹990.00\",\n    \"CGST @ 9%: 990.00\",\n    \"Central GST 9.0% : 990.00\",\n    \"CGST: 990.00\",\n    \"CGST 990.00\",\n    \"CGST: ₹1,980.00\",\n    \"CGST @ 9%: 1,624.95\",\n    \"LED Tube Light 20W\",\n    \"Pack of 20\"\n]\n\nfor s in samples:\n    print(f\"{s!r:30} -> {parse_val(s)}\")\n'","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Verify tax extraction on sample lines"}}]}
