ax` calculation sums CGST, SGST, and IGST, ensuring alignment with the audit logic that flags any CGST or SGST values greater than zero when they should be zero. The next step is to examine the event trigger and its effects.\n\n\nThe event listener retrieves the GST rate from the button, calculates the tax amount from the subtotal, and determines intra-state status based on GSTIN state codes.  If intra-state, CGST and SGST are split evenly, IGST is zero. If inter-state, CGST and SGST are zero, and IGST is the full tax amount.  The `onUpdateHeader` function then updates the invoice header with the new tax values. Examining `app.js`, the values are correctly assigned to the invoice object when \"gst\\_preset\" is received by the header updater. However, further analysis is required to understand the specific behavior when the buyer GSTIN is missing.\n\n\nThe `handleUpdateItem` function updates individual line items, triggering a recomputation of everything using `recomputeEverything()`. Examination of this function confirms that editing item descriptions, quantities, rates, or amounts triggers a recalculation, which is followed by an audit of the invoice. It's imperative to verify that changes to items correctly update the subtotal, which is used in all tax calculations.\n\n\n`auditInvoice` computes the sum of items, and the subtotal is either from the invoice object or the sum of line items. The event listener calculates tax based on the subtotal and applies the tax rate and GST type accordingly. This process updates the tax values in the invoice header.\n\n`recomputeEverything()` updates the scorecard and verifies total values. The scorecard correctly displays CGST, SGST, IGST, total tax, and the total including items and taxes, using values derived from the invoice's subtotal and the item totals.\n\n\nThe event listener for the \"18% (Electrical)\" preset is triggered; it updates the header with preset tax values, followed by a recomputation and re-rendering of the