s. The next step is to unify the GSTIN state code defaulting to ensure the audit scorecard, tax calculations, and intra/inter-state determinations consistently use the same value when the buyer GSTIN is missing from the invoice object.\n\n\nThe parser extracts the buyer GSTIN with a regular expression, and if not found, it tries a fallback logic to extract it from the header or the whole text, excluding the supplier's GSTIN. Finally, a default GSTIN is used if none is found. It's crucial to trace how the default \"06CORPP2712A1Z1\" is handled consistently with the tax logic. Now, reviewing the tax calculation and audit logic, which needs to accommodate this defaulting strategy.\n\n\nAnalysis shows the text parser extracts tax amounts (CGST, SGST, IGST) from specific lines using regular expressions. Regex patterns explicitly target lines containing \"CGST,\" \"SGST,\" or \"IGST,\" ensuring tax amounts are extracted accurately. The code avoids extracting amounts from lines containing \"subtotal\" or \"total in words,\" preventing incorrect tax value extraction from these lines.\n\nThe text parser auto-determines Intra-State vs. Inter-State based on GSTIN state code comparison. If the first two digits of the supplier and buyer GSTINs match, it's intra-state; otherwise, it's inter-state. The absence of a buyer GSTIN triggers a default code. This logic is crucial for correct tax calculation.\n\n\nThe code determines the tax split based on supplier and buyer state codes, applying default 9% CGST/SGST for intra-state and 18% IGST for inter-state if no taxes are extracted. It correctly handles the scenario where explicit tax lines are present in the invoice, ensuring the total tax calculation remains accurate and that CGST, SGST, and IGST values are set based on the calculated tax splits and extracted taxes. The logic adheres to the expected tax calculations for both intra and inter-state scenarios. If explicit tax lines are present in the text of the invoice, the extracted values will take precedence over the defaults in either case.\n\n\nExtracted tax values (CGST, SGST) are being directly used, even when `isIntraState` is true and `IGST` should be zero, indicating a potential logic bypass. Code analysis of `clean_items.py` reveals independent processing of `cgst`, `sgst`, and `igst` values, without enforcing the intra-state zero-IGST rule.\n\nThe addition of `buyer_gstin` to `ExtractedInvoice` and associated prompt schema in `server.py` is being investigated to integrate consistent state code checks within item cleaning logic. The goal is to enforce tax consistency based on GSTIN state comparisons. The next step involves implementing the intra-state tax rule.\n\n\nThe `server.py` implementation now includes explicit defaulting for missing buyer GSTINs and enforces IGST zeroing for intra-state scenarios. The defaulting logic uses \"06CORPP2712A1Z1\" when the buyer GSTIN is missing. A comparison of supplier and buyer state codes, taken from the first two digits of the GSTIN, ensures the correct tax split is applied. Default tax rates are assigned (9% CGST/SGST or 18% IGST) where values are missing. The tax split handling now defaults to CGST, SGST where appropriate, and IGST calculation ensures that the defaults are properly applied.\n\n\nThe core logic now consistently enforces intra-state tax rules across all components, guaranteeing IGST is zero and applying the appropriate tax rates. This addresses the constraint to correct GST calculations without modifying other backend or frontend components. The text parser, audit engine, and audit scorecard components now work in harmony with this updated tax logic. Specifically, the audit scorecard's preset button correctly triggers recalculations and updates the display.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"read_file","args":{"EndLine":485,"Path":"/Users/pranit405/.gemini/antigravity/scratch/audit-it/utils/invoiceTextParser.js","StartLine":430}}]}
